            "id": "D3-02",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Constitutional AI (a)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** The provider trained the model with Constitutional AI and discloses its constitution, which includes principles against validating harmful beliefs. Using independently designed scenarios for the risk, the expert finds that the model's responses conform to those principles at a high rate.\n\n**Claim.** In the evaluated scenarios, the model's behaviour conforms to the stated principles.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that the evidence adequately supports the claim?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-03",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Constitutional AI (b)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** As in D3-02.\n\n**Inference.** The model will respond appropriately in unanticipated situations within the same risk domain (for example, rephrased requests or unfamiliar, vulnerable users), i.e. it has acquired the relevant competence rather than scenario-specific conformity.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that this inference is justified?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-04",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "AI safety via debate (a)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** On a set of disputed questions relevant to the risk scenario, each with an independently established reference answer, judges who read debate transcripts between two model instances reached the reference answer more often than judges given a single-advocate baseline.\n\n**Claim.** On these questions, debate improves judges' accuracy.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that the evidence adequately supports the claim?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-05",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "AI safety via debate (b)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** As in D3-04.\n\n**Inference.** On a contested normative question with **no** reference answer, the position that wins the debate can be treated as a justified resolution of that question.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that this inference is justified?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-06",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Inverse reinforcement learning (a)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** The provider applied inverse reinforcement learning to human demonstrations of desirable conduct in the risk scenario. The expert confirms that the recovered reward function predicts held-out demonstrations well under the stated modelling assumptions.\n\n**Claim.** Under the stated assumptions, the recovered reward is consistent with the demonstrated conduct and predicts held-out behaviour.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that the evidence adequately supports the claim?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-07",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Inverse reinforcement learning (b)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** As in D3-06.\n\n**Inference.** The recovered reward identifies the values the demonstrators actually hold, and can therefore serve as evidence of the values the model was aligned to.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that this inference is justified?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-08",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Access to evidence",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "Under EU law as currently in force, the Article 92 expert needs **the complete principle set and feedback/training records, the protocols of any judge- or debate-based evaluation, and samples of deployment interactions for the risk scenario**. The expert has no access powers of their own: access comes through the Commission's powers under Arts. 91–92 of the AI Act and Implementing Regulation (EU) 2026/1755 and, where they apply to components of the AI system built on the model, Arts. 75(1) and 75a. Take into account limits from other EU law, such as the GDPR for deployment interactions.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would conclude that the expert, through the Commission's powers, can obtain these **as of right**, rather than only on terms the provider agrees to?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-09",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Access entitlement",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "Suppose new legislation created a binding **controlled-evaluator-access entitlement** (see the glossary). The appointed expert receives the material in D3-08 directly, in a secure environment, artefacts are preserved for re-analysis, and results can be shared with the AI Office and, under confidentiality, with a **qualified challenger**, notwithstanding the confidentiality rules of Art. 78.",
            "question": "With this entitlement **alone**, how many of 100 reviewers would judge the D3 anchor claim **adequately verifiable**?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D3-10",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Access entitlement plus disclosure",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "Suppose that, **in addition** to the entitlement in D3-09, the provider must submit, beyond the documentation Annex XI already requires, a **normative-specification disclosure**. It states the principles or reference criteria used for the risk scenario, how conflicts between principles were resolved, which scenarios and user populations were tested, and a procedure through which qualified parties can challenge the specification.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would now judge the D3 anchor claim **adequately verifiable**?"
          }
