            "id": "D4-02",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Constitutional classifiers (a)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** The deployed configuration includes input/output (exchange) classifiers trained from a constitution. Under a declared protocol (attacker access, budget, grader, success threshold, date), the expert's red team runs extended adaptive attacks against that exact configuration and finds no universal jailbreak meeting the success criterion.\n\n**Claim.** Under the declared conditions, the deployed configuration resisted universal jailbreaks for the targeted CBRN categories.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that the evidence adequately supports the claim?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-03",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Constitutional classifiers (b)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** As in D4-02.\n\n**Inference.** No cost-effective universal jailbreak exists for this configuration.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that this inference is justified?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-04",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Circuit breakers (a)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** The provider trained circuit breakers into the model. Using attacks adapted to the defence, including attacks designed with knowledge of how it works, the expert finds harmful-completion rates substantially lower than for the same model without circuit breakers.\n\n**Claim.** Circuit breakers reduce harmful-completion rates against the tested adaptive attacks.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that the evidence adequately supports the claim?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-05",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Circuit breakers (b)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** As in D4-04.\n\n**Inference.** Suppressing the targeted internal representations has removed the harmful capability, rather than redirecting how it can be expressed.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that this inference is justified?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-06",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Process reward modelling (a)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** The provider trained the model with a process reward model that includes safety criteria for each reasoning step. On the tested prompts, the expert observes far fewer written reasoning traces in which the model reasons its way towards complying with a harmful request.\n\n**Claim.** On the tested prompts, the model's written reasoning towards harmful compliance has decreased.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that the evidence adequately supports the claim?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-07",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Process reward modelling (b)",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "**Evidence.** As in D4-06.\n\n**Inference.** The cleaner reasoning traces show that the model no longer has an unsafe policy, rather than that it has learned to keep that policy out of its written reasoning.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would accept that this inference is justified?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-08",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Access to evidence",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "Under EU law as currently in force, the Article 92 expert needs **the deployed guarded configuration** (model version, classifier thresholds, system prompt, tools), **unrestricted adaptive-attack access with knowledge of the defence design**, and **the provider's own red-teaming and grading records**. The expert has no access powers of their own: access comes through the Commission's powers under Arts. 91–92 of the AI Act and Implementing Regulation (EU) 2026/1755 and, where they apply to components of the AI system built on the model, Arts. 75(1) and 75a.",
            "question": "How many of 100 reviewers would conclude that the expert, through the Commission's powers, can obtain these **as of right**, rather than only on terms the provider agrees to?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-09",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Access entitlement",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "Suppose new legislation created a binding **controlled-evaluator-access entitlement** (see the glossary). The appointed expert receives the access and records in D4-08 directly, in a secure environment, configurations and graded outcomes are preserved for re-analysis, and results can be shared with the AI Office and, under confidentiality, with a **qualified challenger**, notwithstanding the confidentiality rules of Art. 78.",
            "question": "With this entitlement **alone**, how many of 100 reviewers would judge the D4 anchor claim **adequately verifiable**?"
          },
          {
            "id": "D4-10",
            "type": "estimate",
            "label": "Access entitlement plus disclosure",
            "required": true,
            "stem": "Suppose that, **in addition** to the entitlement in D4-09, every robustness claim must be accompanied, beyond the documentation Annex XI already requires, by a **declared threat model**. It states the attacker's access tier (query, fine-tuning or weights), the attacker's knowledge of the defence, budget, success criterion and grader, the configuration and date tested, and whether attacks were adapted to the defence. It also includes a duty to re-test after any material change to the configuration.",
